1
00:00:00,000 --> 00:00:10,217
YA-NEC course, lesson 1: a 14 MHz half-wave dipole, built from scratch and simulated in free space, then 5 m and 10 m above average ground.

2
00:00:10,267 --> 00:00:13,780
Start with New: an empty model.

3
00:00:13,830 --> 00:00:19,609
Open Frequency and sweep and type the working frequency: 14.150 MHz.

4
00:00:19,659 --> 00:00:24,785
Sweep span 350 kHz, enough to cover the whole 20 m band.

5
00:00:24,835 --> 00:00:31,652
Classic formula: length = 142.5 / f = 142.5 / 14.15 = 10.07 m, so about 5.03 m per side.

6
00:00:31,702 --> 00:00:36,052
Add wire: one row in the wire table, like in MMANA.

7
00:00:36,102 --> 00:00:47,053
The dipole lies along Y, centred on the origin: from Y = -5.03 m to Y = +5.03 m, at Z = 0.

8
00:00:47,103 --> 00:00:54,320
Radius 1 mm (a 2 mm wire) and 21 segments: an odd number puts a segment exactly in the centre.

9
00:00:54,370 --> 00:00:58,883
Apply and recalculate.

10
00:00:58,933 --> 00:01:06,617
The program warns: the model has no source yet. The Sources table below is where we feed the antenna.

11
00:01:06,667 --> 00:01:14,721
Type w1c: wire 1, centre. Then Apply and recalculate.

12
00:01:14,771 --> 00:01:23,521
Here are the results: impedance about 67 - j32 ohm. The negative reactance tells us the dipole is electrically short.

13
00:01:23,571 --> 00:01:31,055
The 142.5/f formula is only a starting point: a thin wire in free space resonates a little longer.

14
00:01:31,105 --> 00:01:40,239
Lengthen both ends to 5.14 m and recalculate.

15
00:01:40,289 --> 00:01:49,039
Now 72 - j0.3 ohm: resonant. SWR 1.44 on 50 ohm, gain 2.14 dBi: the textbook free-space dipole. Final length 10.28 m.

16
00:01:49,089 --> 00:01:52,440
Patterns tab: azimuth and elevation.

17
00:01:52,490 --> 00:02:03,009
In free space the dipole has the classic figure-eight: two lobes broadside, deep nulls off the ends.

18
00:02:03,059 --> 00:02:08,847
Now the real world: Ground, choose Average (er 13, sigma 0.005 S/m).

19
00:02:08,897 --> 00:02:16,816
The antenna is at Z = 0, on the ground: type the height, 5 m.

20
00:02:16,866 --> 00:02:26,682
At 5 m (a quarter wave) the ground changes everything: 80 + j17 ohm, 5.83 dBi, but the maximum points almost straight up, 69 degrees.

21
00:02:26,732 --> 00:02:30,242
Look at the elevation pattern.

22
00:02:30,292 --> 00:02:40,810
Almost round in azimuth and a big lobe overhead: good for short-range NVIS contacts, poor for DX.

23
00:02:40,860 --> 00:02:46,787
Raise it to 10 m: just type the new height.

24
00:02:46,837 --> 00:02:55,932
At 10 m (about half a wave): 71 - j11 ohm, SWR 1.49, 7.08 dBi, and the maximum comes down to 29.5 degrees.

25
00:02:55,982 --> 00:02:59,493
Elevation pattern at 10 m.

26
00:02:59,543 --> 00:03:08,493
Low lobe, figure-eight again in azimuth: much better for DX. Ground reflection adds up to about 5 dB over free space.

27
00:03:08,543 --> 00:03:15,493
Height above ground changes impedance, gain and radiation angle. YA-NEC is free: github.com/ic8tem/YA-NEC - 73 de IC8TEM
